  "plan": {
    "goal": "Separate cell-mediated drug clearance from tubing/PDMS binding in a perfused liver-on-chip by running the same chip cell-free at the same flow and drug concentration, and report the cell-attributable clearance.",
    "rationale": "Cell-attributable clearance is isolated by subtracting the cell-free (binding) clearance from the full-chip clearance: Cl_cell = Cl_full − Cl_cellfree = 0.45 − 0.05 = 0.40 µL/min. The cell-free chip run at identical flow and inlet concentration captures non-specific tubing/PDMS adsorption (E ≈ 0.033), which is subtracted to leave only cell-mediated extraction. The full chip shows E ≈ 0.30 (30% per pass), consistent with an intermediate-extraction compound. Flow set at 1.5 µL/min per channel; inlet 10 µM. A 200 µL recirculating system volume gives a cell-mediated half-life of ~5.8 h, a practical window for repeat sampling. Assumes identical geometry, flow, temperature, and medium between the two runs so the only difference is the presence of cells.",
